 "blocks": [
  {
   "t": "notice",
   "text": "Draft for Jared's approval. Collected word for word from the city-wide rules paragraphs of the neighbourhood guides listed at the bottom (D-202); nothing new was added. Each guide keeps what is specific to it and links here.",
   "gen": "cityrules"
  },
  {
   "t": "p",
   "text": "Buying anywhere in Chilliwack? These are the rules that apply city-wide, by topic: property taxes, transfer tax and GST, floodplain, farmland and environmental rules, strata, co-op and age rules and zoning, suites and small-scale housing. The neighbourhood guides cover what is specific to each area. Rules change, so check anything that matters for your purchase with the City of Chilliwack and your lawyer before you remove subjects.",
   "gen": "cityrules"
  },
  {
   "t": "h",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Property taxes, transfer tax and GST in Chilliwack",
   "id": "taxes",
   "gen": "cityrules"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "Subdivision within the ALR requires Agricultural Land Commission approval; ALR residences cannot be stratified (ALC Bulletin 09 s.6; SPA s.239; alca s.21(1)); residence caps as the ALC states them: Principal residence 500 m² or less, a suite within the principal residence only, and one additional residence of 90 m² or less on parcels 40 ha or smaller and 186 m² or less on parcels larger than 40 ha. Parcel-level ALR status is per address on ALC mapping. Floodplain Regulation Bylaw 2018, No. 4519 sets minimum building setbacks of 60 m from the Fraser River, 75 m from the Vedder River and 30 m from the Chilliwack River and Vedder Canal; the City maintains a diking network for the Fraser and Vedder systems and levies drainage and dyking taxes on top of municipal rates."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "taxes"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "Chilliwack property taxes: notices are mailed annually at the end of May and taxes are due the first working day after July 1 of every calendar year; a 5% penalty applies for taxes outstanding after July 2, 2026 and an additional 5% for taxes outstanding after August 4, 2026. This staged 5% + 5% is Chilliwack's own city scheme — do not confuse it with Mission's flat 10%, or with the rural Taxation (Rural Area) Act regime (whose second stage runs at October 31 and is paid to the Province)."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "taxes"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "Hillside development permit areas — the central local rule: Development Permit Area 2 (Hillside and Upland Areas) protects developments from hazardous hillside conditions and environmentally sensitive areas, and a Geotechnical Report from a qualified professional is required with each application; Development Permit Area 5 (Hillside Development) applies to all lands in the City with slopes of 20% or more, and within a designated DPA a Development Permit is required before subdivision, construction, addition or alteration of a building, or alteration of land, except where exemptions apply."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "taxes"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "Vancouver's Empty Homes Tax at 3% is vancouver only and does not apply here."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "taxes"
  },
  {
   "t": "h",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Floodplain, farmland and environmental rules in Chilliwack",
   "id": "flood",
   "gen": "cityrules"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "Chilliwack plan and zoning: Chilliwack 2050 Official Community Plan, Bylaw No. 5500, with the Agricultural Area Plan as Schedule B1 (the Sardis plan is Schedule B5 of the same bylaw — never \"Schedule D / Bylaw 5024\"). Zoning is Zoning Bylaw 2020, No. 5000: in the AL (Agricultural Lowland) zone the minimum lot area is 7.5 ha for land excluded or exempted from the ALR or approved for subdivision within the ALR, 1 ha for a boundary adjustment, and 0.2 ha for a homesite severance or boundary adjustment approved by the Agricultural Land Commission; minimum lot width is 25 m. Smaller residential lots exist near the school and along Chilliwack Central Road. Chilliwack's SSMUH bylaw is Bylaw 5395, adopted June 18, 2024."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "flood"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "Geotechnical rules: steep slopes, main watercourses and ravines cross the plan area and the plan identifies geotechnical hazard areas; the 2017 plan amendments were made specifically to protect future development from rock fall, landslide or earthflow with unstable conditions, and Council directed an expanded Marble Hill development moratorium area in 2017 after continuing ground movement was reported. The City's Greenspace Plan identifies the lower and upper Marble Hill slide areas as undevelopable lands. Get the property's geotechnical reports and any covenants on title before pricing or removing subjects, and check whether the lot sits in the moratorium area before counting on subdivision or a build."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "flood"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "Plan and zoning: Slope decides the lot math: in SHR the minimum lot area rises with the slope band (360 m² up to 990 m²), so a \"big\" hillside lot may not subdivide the way its area suggests."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "flood"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "Hillside rules that hit townhouse owners directly: DPA D-2 covers the whole mountain, and a development permit with a geotechnical hazard and slope assessment is required before subdivision, construction, land clearing or earthworks — the rule behind every addition, deck or rebuild conversation in a mountain strata."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "flood"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "Confirm flood construction level, setbacks and floodplain position with the City before removing subjects, especially near the Vedder River, Vedder Canal or a creek."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "flood"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "Flood: Confirm the parcel's own flood construction level and floodplain position with the City before removing subjects."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "flood"
  },
  {
   "t": "h",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Strata, co-op and age rules in Chilliwack",
   "id": "strata",
   "gen": "cityrules"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "SSMUH applies to eligible single-family lots, not to lots inside an existing strata townhouse complex."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "strata"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "Inside a strata townhouse, the strata's bylaws govern first."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "strata"
  },
  {
   "t": "h",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Zoning, suites and small-scale housing in Chilliwack",
   "id": "zoning",
   "gen": "cityrules"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "SSMUH / suites / accessory dwellings (Chilliwack specifics): the SSMUH amendment is ESTABLISHED — Zoning Bylaw Amendment Bylaw 2024, No. 5395 amended Zoning Bylaw 2020, No. 5000 and was adopted June 18, 2024, as recorded in s. 14's amendment record in the consolidated Zoning Bylaw 5000 ( item 6). Suite/ADU figures confirmed in the same verification (item 7f, citing Zoning Bylaw 5000, ss. 2, 4.06, 5.04): a secondary suite is capped at the lesser of 90 m² and 40% of the principal dwelling, one accessory dwelling unit is permitted per lot, garden suites are capped at 100 m², and parking rules sit at s. 5.04. Application to any specific parcel remains a per-parcel check."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "zoning"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "OCP: Chilliwack 2050, Official Community Plan Bylaw 2025, No. 5500 — current: Schedule B3 is the Downtown Neighbourhood Plan (adopted June 2010, updated 2025, per the plan record read via search result) articulating the vision for the Downtown Plan Area over 30 years, and Schedule D-6 is the Downtown development permit area, whose guidelines exist to facilitate the Downtown Design Guidelines under s.488(1)(d) of the Local Government Act and to support revitalization of the historic downtown core."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "zoning"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "SSMUH (Bylaw 5395, adopted Jun 18, 2024): Zoning Bylaw Amendment Bylaw 2024, No. 5395, amending Bylaw 5000, allows 3 to 4 dwelling units on parcels within the urban growth boundary zoned for single-detached and duplex housing."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "zoning"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "Zoning / subdivision / servicing: Subdivision runs through the City's Approving Officer and the Preliminary Layout Approval process: confirm the parcel's zone, subdivision requirements, servicing and frontage conditions with the Approving Officer for the PID before valuing a split."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "zoning"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "Farm character the plan protects: the Agricultural Area Plan states the farm sector in the plan area is dominated by dairy and poultry operations, nurseries and greenhouses."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "zoning"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "Plan and zoning: Confirm the parcel's zone section before assuming any farm or home-based use is permitted."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "zoning"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "Confirm the parcel's zone before assuming any farm or residential use."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "zoning"
  },
  {
   "t": "h",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Other things to know about buying in Chilliwack",
   "id": "other",
   "gen": "cityrules"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "Short-term rentals (Chilliwack): STR operations are regulated under Short-Term Rental Bylaw 2021, No. 5141, which repealed the former Bed and Breakfast Bylaw 2003, No. 2968 — confirm a property can be licensed under it before valuing any rental income from a lot purchase."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "other"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "Pad tenure (where met — Country Park Village): a manufactured home on a rented pad is not lot stock and not land ownership. The park's own lease/tenancy documents govern."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "other"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "Board: Chilliwack is CADREB ground."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "other"
  },
